  "preamble": "Fronting of the open-mid back rounded vowel [ɔ] to the close-mid front rounded [ø]. Around the 13th century Old Norse ǫ /ɔ/, the u-umlaut of a, merged with /ø/ or /o/ in most dialects and with /ø/ in Icelandic; Old Danish kept it apart. Faroese writes the merged vowel ø, long [øː] and short [œ]. In Icelandic the merged vowel is written ö and has since opened to [œ].",
